\f1\b\fs36 \ul Some of my personal tips for use in Draw.io:
\f0\b0\fs24 \ulnone \
\
\
\pard\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf0 \ul Use the Layers function
\f0\b0\fs24 \ulnone \
\
Much like Photoshop or other programs, you can create Layers to separate elements. Just go up to \'93View\'94 and select \'93Layers,\'94 and a layers popup menu will show up that allows you to add, lock and rearrange their order. Personally, I like to start off creating a \'93Foundation\'94 layer to lay out my block grid, then a \'93Buildings\'94 layer for my builds and connection lines, and often an additional \'93Labels\'94 layer and even a \'93Power Lines\'94 layer. But you can do whatever works for you.\
\
Using layers to separate groupings of objects can help you clear out visual noise if you\'92re trying to proof your final design. You can click off the foundation layer, for example, and just look at the buildings, and vice-versa. Have a layer just for the power lines, and turn those off so you can remove the red-line clutter to see your diagram more clearly, and so on.\
\

\f1\b \ul \

\fs28 You can label elements
\fs24 \
\pard\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0 \cf0 \ulnone \
Even though the icons have their own little text labels telling you what they are, you can double-click on anything and type in your own information (that\'92s how I can label my storage containers to denote what\'92s actually stored in them).\
\
\pard\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 \ul \

\fs28 Change your belt colors to denote altitude
\f0\b0\fs24 \ulnone \
\
The conveyor belt line I\'92ve used here is a dark grey, but it can be changed like everything else in the program to match your preferences. I like to use the default grey as a ground-level conveyor belt. If I raise the belt up by a level (like the second tier to an industrial container), I tend to lighten the color a notch and make sure it\'92s arranged to the top above any ground-level conveyor lines. If a third higher tier is needed, I use a lighter grey, and so on.\
\

\f1\b \ul \

\fs28 Conveyor Lift Icons:
\fs24 \
\pard\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0 \cf0 \ulnone \
If the Conveyor Lift icons are a little confusing, let me clarify: I defined an \'91outgoing\'92 icon as one I drop in if that part of the lift is collecting and taking something away from the belt or producer it\'92s connected to. I use the \'93incoming\'94 icon if it\'92s bringing something from its other end to this next connection.\
\
Personally, I like to give my Conveyor Lift icons a number (you can double click on the icon and then type it in to superimpose it over the image).\
\
By giving the conveyor lift a number, I can then go to create a new page for a new tier/level of my build. Then when I drop the corresponding other end of that conveyor lift there, I can put its corresponding number on it. \
\
By doing this, I know where a given conveyor lift from one tier\'92s layout ends up going in the next tier (so Conveyor Lift 1, or Conveyor Lift Iron Rods, whatever you want to call it, has its matching output/input show up in the correct spot on whatever other tier/layer you have it on).\
\
\pard\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 \ul \

\fs28 Power Lines
\f0\b0\fs24 \ulnone \
\
Since draw io always assumes whatever you\'92ve just put down is the topmost item, sometimes as you start editing and adding new elements your old ones will get covered up. \
\
This especially happens with Power Lines. Anytime you see that your power line has gotten something put overtop it, just click on the line, go up to \'93Arrange -> Bring to Front\'94 and you\'92re fine. You can use Edit -> Lock to fix the object while it\'92s selected, but this only tends to lock the location, not its order in the layout.\
\
You can also create a new Layer in the document (just like Photoshop or other image programs) and put your Power Lines there instead. This is suggested because then you can always turn off the layer if you want to look at your layout without the distraction of all those lines cutting across it at crazy angles.\
\
